Skip is the right pattern for **predictable bad records** in dirty input data. The framework counts skips and enforces a tolerance; the listener writes the skipped item somewhere durable for audit.

**Basic skip configuration**

```java
@Bean
public Step importStep(JobRepository repo, PlatformTransactionManager tx,
                       ItemReader<Order> reader,
                       ItemProcessor<Order, Invoice> processor,
                       ItemWriter<Invoice> writer,
                       SkipListener<Order, Invoice> skipListener) {
    return new StepBuilder("importStep", repo)
            .<Order, Invoice>chunk(500, tx)
            .reader(reader)
            .processor(processor)
            .writer(writer)
            .faultTolerant()
                .skip(FlatFileParseException.class)
                .skip(ValidationException.class)
                .skipLimit(500)
                .listener(skipListener)
            .build();
}
```

**SkipListener for dead-letter handling**

```java
@Component
public class DeadLetterSkipListener implements SkipListener<Order, Invoice> {
    private final KafkaTemplate<String, byte[]> kafka;

    @Override
    public void onSkipInRead(Throwable t) {
        kafka.send("batch-skips", "READ", serialize(Map.of(
            "phase", "READ", "error", t.getMessage())));
    }
    @Override public void onSkipInProcess(Order item, Throwable t) {
        kafka.send("batch-skips", item.getId(), serialize(item, t));
    }
    @Override public void onSkipInWrite(Invoice item, Throwable t) {
        kafka.send("batch-skips", item.getOrderId(), serialize(item, t));
    }
}
```

**Per-exception limits with `LimitCheckingItemSkipPolicy`**
- Different limits for different exception classes (1000 parse errors, 10 DB integrity errors)
- More precise than the global `skipLimit`

```java
Map<Class<? extends Throwable>, Boolean> exceptions = Map.of(
    FlatFileParseException.class, true,
    DataIntegrityViolationException.class, true);
LimitCheckingItemSkipPolicy policy = new LimitCheckingItemSkipPolicy();
policy.setSkipLimit(500);
policy.setSkippableExceptionMap(exceptions);
```

**Skip pitfalls**
- **`skipLimit(Integer.MAX_VALUE)`** silently corrupts data; use a real number
- **`skip(Exception.class)`** masks real bugs like NullPointerException
- **No `SkipListener`** — skipped items vanish; can't reprocess or audit
- **Skipping on non-idempotent processors** — side effects fired before the skip

Retry without backoff makes transient failures worse. If 100 concurrent chunk writers all retry immediately after a deadlock, they retry into the same contention.

**Basic retry configuration**

```java
.faultTolerant()
    .retry(DeadlockLoserDataAccessException.class)
    .retry(TransientDataAccessException.class)
    .retryLimit(3)
    .backOffPolicy(exponentialBackoff())
```

**Exponential backoff policy**

```java
@Bean
public ExponentialBackOffPolicy exponentialBackoff() {
    ExponentialBackOffPolicy policy = new ExponentialBackOffPolicy();
    policy.setInitialInterval(500);
    policy.setMultiplier(2.0);
    policy.setMaxInterval(10_000);
    return policy;
}
```

First retry after 500ms, second after 1s, third after 2s — capped at 10s.

**RetryListener for observability**

```java
@Component
public class MetricsRetryListener implements RetryListener {
    private final MeterRegistry meter;
    @Override public <T, E extends Throwable> void onError(
            RetryContext ctx, RetryCallback<T, E> cb, Throwable t) {
        meter.counter("batch.retry",
                "exception", t.getClass().getSimpleName(),
                "attempt", String.valueOf(ctx.getRetryCount())).increment();
    }
    @Override public <T, E extends Throwable> void close(
            RetryContext ctx, RetryCallback<T, E> cb, Throwable t) {
        if (t != null) meter.counter("batch.retry.exhausted").increment();
    }
}
```

**Job-level retry — outside Spring Batch**
- Spring Batch has no built-in job-level retry
- The scheduler (Quartz, Kubernetes CronJob) re-launches a `FAILED` job after a delay
- Pair with `RunIdIncrementer` so each retry creates a new `JobExecution` against the same `JobInstance`

**Retry pitfalls**
- **Retry on non-transient errors** masks real bugs and inflates wall-clock time
- **No backoff** — retry storms compound the original failure
- **Retry on a stateful processor** — internal state may not be reset between attempts
- **RetryLimit too high** — turns transient failure into a long delay before the user sees anything

Before writing policies, classify the exception classes the job can encounter. The classification drives the configuration.

**Transient — retry candidates**
- `DeadlockLoserDataAccessException` — Spring's wrapper for DB deadlock
- `TransientDataAccessException` — superclass for transient JDBC errors
- `CannotAcquireLockException` — JDBC lock wait timeout
- `ResourceAccessException` (Spring REST) — connection refused, timeout
- `KafkaTimeoutException` — broker temporarily unavailable
- HTTP 5xx with retryable status codes

**Permanent data — skip candidates**
- `FlatFileParseException` — malformed CSV row
- `ValidationException` — Bean Validation failure
- `DataIntegrityViolationException` — UNIQUE or NOT NULL violation (sometimes — see below)
- `IncorrectTokenCountException` — wrong number of CSV columns
- Custom business validation exceptions

**Fatal — fail fast**
- `NullPointerException` — almost always a bug
- `IllegalStateException` / `IllegalArgumentException` — invariant violation
- `ClassCastException` — type system error
- `OutOfMemoryError` — never recover; fail and let the operator investigate

**The ambiguous middle**
- `DataIntegrityViolationException` — could be a permanent duplicate row (skip) OR a constraint that fires on a transient race condition (retry)
- Resolve by inspecting the exception cause or `getMessage()` and routing accordingly

```java
public class IntegritySkipDecider implements SkipPolicy {
    @Override
    public boolean shouldSkip(Throwable t, long skipCount) {
        if (t instanceof DataIntegrityViolationException dive) {
            String msg = dive.getMessage() == null ? "" : dive.getMessage().toLowerCase();
            if (msg.contains("unique") || msg.contains("duplicate key")) return true;
            return false;
        }
        if (t instanceof FlatFileParseException) return true;
        return false;
    }
}
```

`SimpleRetryPolicy` retries up to N times for any matching exception. Production usually wants more: different limits per exception class, per-business-key counts, or never-retry-after-N-total rules.

**The RetryPolicy interface**

```java
public interface RetryPolicy {
    boolean canRetry(RetryContext context);
    RetryContext open(RetryContext parent);
    void close(RetryContext context);
    void registerThrowable(RetryContext context, Throwable throwable);
}
```

**Per-exception limits — built-in `ExceptionClassifierRetryPolicy`**

```java
Map<Class<? extends Throwable>, RetryPolicy> policies = Map.of(
    DeadlockLoserDataAccessException.class, new SimpleRetryPolicy(5),
    TransientDataAccessException.class, new SimpleRetryPolicy(3),
    KafkaTimeoutException.class, new SimpleRetryPolicy(10));
ExceptionClassifierRetryPolicy retryPolicy = new ExceptionClassifierRetryPolicy();
retryPolicy.setPolicyMap(policies);
```

**Custom RetryPolicy — circuit-breaker behavior**

```java
public class CircuitBreakerRetryPolicy extends SimpleRetryPolicy {
    private final AtomicLong totalFailures = new AtomicLong();
    private final long globalFailureLimit;

    public CircuitBreakerRetryPolicy(int retryLimit, long globalFailureLimit) {
        super(retryLimit);
        this.globalFailureLimit = globalFailureLimit;
    }

    @Override
    public boolean canRetry(RetryContext context) {
        if (totalFailures.get() >= globalFailureLimit) return false;
        return super.canRetry(context);
    }

    @Override
    public void registerThrowable(RetryContext context, Throwable t) {
        totalFailures.incrementAndGet();
        super.registerThrowable(context, t);
    }
}
```

Wiring into the step requires building a `RetryTemplate` and using a `RetryOperationsInterceptor` (advanced); most use cases are met with `ExceptionClassifierRetryPolicy` plus a custom `BackOffPolicy`.

**RetryPolicy vs BackOffPolicy**
- `RetryPolicy` decides **whether** to retry
- `BackOffPolicy` decides **how long to wait** before retrying
- They compose — `ExponentialBackOffPolicy` with `ExceptionClassifierRetryPolicy` is a common pairing

Production skip policies often encode business risk tolerance more precisely than absolute counts. A `PercentageSkipPolicy` is the most common upgrade from `SimpleLimitSkipPolicy`.

**The SkipPolicy interface**

```java
public interface SkipPolicy {
    boolean shouldSkip(Throwable t, long skipCount) throws SkipLimitExceededException;
}
```

**Percentage-based tolerance**

```java
@Component
@StepScope
public class PercentageSkipPolicy implements SkipPolicy {
    private final StepExecution stepExecution;
    private final double maxSkipFraction;
    private final long warmupItems;

    public PercentageSkipPolicy(@Value("#{stepExecution}") StepExecution stepExecution,
                                @Value("#{jobParameters['maxSkipPercent']}") Double maxPct,
                                @Value("#{jobParameters['warmupItems']}") Long warmup) {
        this.stepExecution = stepExecution;
        this.maxSkipFraction = maxPct == null ? 0.05 : maxPct;
        this.warmupItems = warmup == null ? 1000 : warmup;
    }

    @Override
    public boolean shouldSkip(Throwable t, long skipCount) {
        if (!isSkippable(t)) return false;
        long readCount = stepExecution.getReadCount();
        if (readCount < warmupItems) return true;
        return ((double) skipCount / readCount) <= maxSkipFraction;
    }

    private boolean isSkippable(Throwable t) {
        return t instanceof FlatFileParseException || t instanceof ValidationException;
    }
}
```

If 5% of items have failed by the time you're past the warmup window, the data is too dirty to trust — fail fast.

**Tiered per-exception SkipPolicy**

```java
public class TieredSkipPolicy implements SkipPolicy {
    private final AtomicLong parseSkips = new AtomicLong();
    private final AtomicLong validationSkips = new AtomicLong();
    private final long parseSkipLimit;
    private final long validationSkipLimit;

    public TieredSkipPolicy(long parseSkipLimit, long validationSkipLimit) {
        this.parseSkipLimit = parseSkipLimit;
        this.validationSkipLimit = validationSkipLimit;
    }

    @Override
    public boolean shouldSkip(Throwable t, long skipCount) {
        if (t instanceof FlatFileParseException) {
            return parseSkips.incrementAndGet() <= parseSkipLimit;
        }
        if (t instanceof ValidationException) {
            return validationSkips.incrementAndGet() <= validationSkipLimit;
        }
        return false;
    }
}
```

**Pair every SkipPolicy with a SkipListener**
- The policy decides whether to skip; the listener decides what to do with the skipped item
- Always write to a durable destination (Kafka topic, file, audit table) for human review
- Without this, you've created a silent data-loss vector

**SkipPolicy pitfalls**
- **Stateful counters not thread-safe** — multi-threaded steps need `AtomicLong`
- **Reading `StepExecution.getReadCount()` mid-skip** — count is updated lazily; understand the timing
- **No warmup window** — divide-by-zero or false-positive on the first failure
- **Logic that depends on transaction state** — chunks roll back; skip counts may diverge from your mental model
